<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Duala people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Duala are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> coast, living mainly around the Wouri estuary and the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douala">Douala</a>. From the 17th century their chiefs, notably the Bell and Akwa lineages, grew rich as middlemen in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic trade</a> in slaves, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a>. In 1884 their kings signed the treaty that made the estuary the core of German Kamerun.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/duala-people.jpg" alt="Duala people" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Happiraphael · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Danse_sawa8.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Early Duala history is known only from oral tradition. The Duala trace their descent to Mbedi, who lived at Bakota in what is now Gabon or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a>. His sons Ewale and Dibongo migrated north to Pitti on the Dibamba River and parted there after a quarrel: Ewale's followers settled on the east bank of the Wouri estuary and became the Duala, while Dibongo's became the Limba. According to Duala tradition, the Bakoko and Bassa held the estuary when they arrived and were driven inland, probably in the late 17th or early 18th century. The Duala belong to the Sawa, the coastal peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Duala bought goods and slaves from inland peoples such as the Bakweri, Mungo, Bassa and Bakoko and sold them to Europeans for alcohol, guns, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>, textiles and tools. In the 19th century Ndumb'a Lobe of the Bell lineage styled himself King Bell, and rivals arose, among them King Akwa in 1814 and King Deido. Akwa and Bell signed anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> treaties with the British in 1840 and 1841, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-saker">Alfred Saker</a> opened a mission at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douala">Douala</a> in 1845. In July 1884 the German explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-nachtigal">Gustav Nachtigal</a> secured treaties ceding land from Kings Akwa, Bell and Deido.</p><p>A literate class of clerks, farmers and traders resisted unpopular German measures with petitions and lawsuits. When the administration tried from 1910 to seize land in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douala">Douala</a> and remove its people, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-duala-manga-bell">Rudolf Duala Manga Bell</a> rallied opposition, and he was executed for high treason in 1914. Under the French mandate after 1918, almost half of the 15–20,000 Duala were traders, planters, chiefs or clerks by the 1930s, but Bamileke migrants made them a minority in Douala, and many supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-the-peoples-of-cameroon">Union des Populations du Cameroun</a> when it formed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Duala live mainly in the Moungo, Nkam and Wouri divisions of the Littoral, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douala">Douala</a> is their traditional capital. Many in the city live from rents on land they own, while rural Duala fish and farm. Inheritance passes through the male line. Most have been Christian since the 1930s, chiefly Baptist, though belief in ancestors who dwell in the sea and in water spirits called Miengu persists. The Ngondo, an assembly of chiefs, and age groups called miemba hold the community together, and pirogue racing is their traditional sport.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Duala people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Duala</b> settled on the Wouri estuary, probably in the 17th century, and by the late 1600s were dealing with European ships that called at the river.</blockquote><a href="/state/duala-kingdoms">Duala kingdoms</a></li><li><blockquote>On 12 July 1884 the <b>Duala</b> kings Bell and Akwa signed a treaty with German agents, and Gustav Nachtigal raised the German flag at the Duala kingdoms days later.</blockquote><a href="/state/kamerun">Kamerun</a></li></ul></section>
